 ## Silence guard
 
-A synthesis can collapse: the model stops speaking and generates near-digital silence until its end of speech or the frame limit. One measured reply did this for 64 s; another stayed silent for 37 s until the frame limit. The model can also stall and then recover: one reply was silent for 7.4 s and then spoke the rest of its text correctly.
+A synthesis can collapse: the model stops speaking and generates silence until its end of speech or the frame limit. In the default voice that silence is near-digital: one measured reply did this for 64 s; another stayed silent for 37 s until the frame limit. It can also be a steady hum: one reply in a model-invented voice spoke for 1.5 s and hummed at about -58 dBFS until it ended at 45.2 s. The model can also stall and then recover: one reply was silent for 7.4 s and then spoke the rest of its text correctly.
 
-So the engine decodes audio while it generates, every 4 frames for a stream and every 16 frames (0.74 s) otherwise. It ends a synthesis only once 10 s of frames in a row are below -60 dBFS (an RMS of 0.001), so a stall that has not recovered by then is treated as a collapse. Shorter stalls are kept as they are: the server does not trim them.
+So the engine decodes audio while it generates, every 4 frames for a stream and every 16 frames (0.74 s) otherwise. It ends a synthesis only once 10 s of frames in a row are below -40 dBFS (an RMS of 0.01), so a stall that has not recovered by then is treated as a collapse. Shorter stalls are kept as they are: the server does not trim them.
 
 - The audio up to the frame that completes the 10 s is kept, the silent run included. That is the same frame for a stream and a whole reply of the same generation: each chunk is checked before a stream's client receives it, and frames decoded past the cut are dropped. The reply carries `X-Finish-Reason: silence`: a header on a whole reply, a trailer on a stream.
 - Silence that completes 10 s only in the last frames still counts. It outranks both the end of speech and the frame limit, because the reply collapsed however generation stopped.
@@ -152,6 +152,10 @@ So the engine decodes audio while it generates, every 4 frames for a stream and
 - **Whole replies decode in chunks of 16 frames,** with the incremental decode that streams use. This was measured on the 48-request default-voice capture against the previous build:
   - All 46 normal replies were byte-identical (two passes of 23), including the one with the recovering 7.4 s stall. 9 of 9 streams matched.
   - The reply with the 37 s silence was cut in both passes at 20.8 s, 10 s into its stall. What remained was a byte-identical prefix of the previous build's audio. That build cut at the end of a 16-frame decode chunk; the guard now cuts at the frame that completes the 10 s, at most 15 frames (0.7 s) earlier.
+- **The -40 dBFS floor** was chosen on 278 renders of the default voice and model-invented voices, replayed offline:
+  - Each of the 11 collapses has 10 s with no frame above -51.7 dBFS. Each of the other 267 renders has a frame at -18.5 dBFS or louder in every 10 s, and none has a run below -40 dBFS longer than the recovering stall (7.5 s at this floor).
+  - A -60 dBFS floor missed one collapse into a hum, whose frames kept rising above -60 dBFS and restarting the run, and ended another only 15.4 s late, once its hum had sunk below -60 dBFS for good. At -40 dBFS each ends 10 s after it began.
+  - Speech quieter than -40 dBFS for 10 s is taken for a collapse.
 
 ## Long-form replies
 
